#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064026 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "b11d3606-7700-5c0f-89da-26b22a0ffd9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-228" :question/problem "A  $15\\,\\textrm{ft}$ ladder is leaning against a wall. If the top of the ladder is sliding down at a rate of $1\\textrm{ ft}/\\textrm{s},$ how fast is the bottom of the ladder moving away from the wall when the top of the ladder is $9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ from the ground?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/9e/9e0fc4dc0e9ed182c4bfdcd3b127f9343fa6030be8c549c77880c6998a265c8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the bottom of the ladder,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the top of the ladder and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the ladder.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}.$ \n\nWe know that $y = 9\\,\\textrm{ft}$ and $z=15\\,\\textrm{ft}.$ We can find $x$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2}+{9}^{2} & ={15}^{2} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =225-81 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =144 \\\\ x & =12\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = -1\\,\\textrm{ft}/\\textrm{s},$\n\n- Note that the length of the ladder remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t} = 0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(9)(-1) & =2(15)(0) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{18}{24} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the ladder is sliding away at a rate of $0.75\\text{ ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064027 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "0284ca7f-ee39-5b9f-87f7-df109aa2200d" :question/math-academy-id "e-229" :question/problem "A rocket is launched vertically and is being tracked by a radar station which is located on the ground $8\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the launch site. If the speed of the rocket is $1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when it is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground, how fast is the distance between the radar station and the rocket increasing?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/8c/8c28dc5302e1f6e8e6ed905737b1487b7a2c6fcea7603c75eecca037ab64ebd1.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the radar station and the launch site,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the rocket and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the radar station and the rocket.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\dfrac{\\textrm d z}{\\textrm d t}.$\n\nWe know that $y = 15\\,\\textrm{km}$ and $x=8\\,\\textrm{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {8}^{2}+{15}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 64+225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 289 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =17\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t} = 1700\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when $y=15,$\n\n- the distance between the radar station and the launch site ($x$) remains the same, so $\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of $x^2+y^2=z^2$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(8)(0)+2(15)(1700) & =2(17)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 51000 & =34\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =1500\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the radar station and the rocket is increasing at a rate of $1500\\,\\textrm{km}/\\textrm{h}$ when the rocket is $15\\,\\textrm{km}$ from the ground."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186238053 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "152f09c1-2b4c-5050-bb9c-533f0063cd43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.7\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235944} {:answer/id #uuid "197976b6-d8c4-5eaa-a2b0-688cc80650c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.8\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235977} {:answer/id #uuid "a5cca184-297c-5a51-8000-69ff9d31794e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236858} {:answer/id #uuid "bfbede8f-435b-56bc-843f-241505817845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237037} {:answer/id #uuid "ec10295d-25b1-5a19-b89f-69f7478d51cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.1\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237304}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bfbede8f-435b-56bc-843f-241505817845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.9\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237037} :answer-field/id #uuid "e41e2628-f143-5669-8c9e-14ea2edbfb3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237729}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2b855eb-fff2-5496-9e40-b074bb54ef82" :question/math-academy-id "q-103792" :question/problem "James and Mary have a date at the cinema theater.\nJames arrived at the agreed time, but Mary is late. After $20$ minutes of waiting, James decides to go home.\n\nJames travels north from the theater with a speed of $3.2\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Mary travels west toward the theater. When James is $0.8\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater, Mary is $1.5\\,\\text{km}$ from the theater. If the distance between them increases at a speed of $2.3\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Mary's speed?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/17/1728c9ca91ef28b19c28048f5a640d0eebbd08f504bb594bc978277fe5620d70.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Mary and the cinema theater,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between James and the cinema theater,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between James and Mary.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=1.5\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=0.8\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {1.5}^{2}+{0.8}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =2.89 \\\\ z & =1.7\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=3.2\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=2.3\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(1.5)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(0.8)(3.2) & =2(1.7)(2.3) \\\\ 3\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =7.82-5.12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Mary is approaching the cinema theater with a speed of $0.9\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521481 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2041b6e3-2c7b-5767-bee7-c0917fa37089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520830} {:answer/id #uuid "5a4b991b-0be0-5421-81c6-155a8e77ad74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520970} {:answer/id #uuid "79307c51-6bb8-5bf4-add1-639696a73890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521036} {:answer/id #uuid "7a90aea4-90dd-561b-8cfe-71c7cd68506b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521040} {:answer/id #uuid "f57c07c6-710c-5e34-b6d0-e037d5b35912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79307c51-6bb8-5bf4-add1-639696a73890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.5\\,\\text{mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521036} :answer-field/id #uuid "2979a513-9e50-5580-a324-a6d842e9ce90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521347}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "385d74bf-db85-520e-88d9-f555fc3d02fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-104077" :question/problem "After attending school, Elizabeth and Jessica leave at the same time. Elizabeth heads north at a speed of $4.5\\text{ mph},$ while Jessica heads east at a speed of $6\\text{ mph}.$ How fast are the two moving away from each other after walking for $2$ hours?" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/2b/2b012a853cf973cc68df35bbd94a498fd1d0d907548c1f6d8ca3f842c967c877.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Jessica and the school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Elizabeth and the school, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine Elizabeth's distance from school and Jessica's distance from school after $2$ hours. Since their velocities are constants, we get \n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=4.5(2)=9\\text{ mi}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot t=6(2)=12\\text{ mi}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {9}^{2}+{12}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 225 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =15\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours,\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{mph}$ after $2$ hours.\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(9)(4.5)+2(12)(6) & =2(15)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 225 & =30\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =7.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between Elizabeth and Jessica increases at a rate of $7.5\\,\\text{mph}$ after walking $2$ hours."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31136b91-ada4-5499-9ab7-4a99b8df1106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.7\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520866} {:answer/id #uuid "770caf56-2690-584a-b68e-89f941399a82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.1\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521029} {:answer/id #uuid "8769679e-53c2-5284-875f-0b62a16bd910" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4.0\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521073} {:answer/id #uuid "8db4ecb8-a27a-582a-ba29-b2b279bd5866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.2\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521086} {:answer/id #uuid "c303aadb-c779-5586-a605-55e86f1bc278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.1\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c303aadb-c779-5586-a605-55e86f1bc278" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.1\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521198} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab01a3c-9c95-5b30-9a80-de234df6bc12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521411}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2bda6d09-1af6-5a1c-86e9-4f34148c24fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-104079" :question/problem "![](images/9d/9dbee02f0c07041dd8424faf683952ada2f7f1a604f9bf89a65588eaf3b6f9f6.png)\n\nThe legs of an isosceles right triangle are increasing. The length, measured in feet, of each leg is given by $x(t)=t+{t}^{2},$ where $t>0$ is the time in seconds. What is the rate of the change of the length of the hypotenuse after $2$ seconds? Give your answer rounded to $1$ decimal place." :question/worked-solution "In the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the equal sides, and\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $x,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nFirst, we determine the length of the equal sides after $2$ seconds. We get \n\n$$\nx=x(2)=(2)+(2)^{2}=6\\text{ ft}.\n$$\n\nWe can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{x}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {6}^{2}+{6}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 72 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & ≈8.485\\,3\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe rate of change of the sides is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(t)=2t+1.$ So, when $t=2$ their velocity is\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}={x}^{′}(2)=2(2)+1=5\\text{ ft/s}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(6)(5)+2(6)(5) & ≈2(8.485\\,3)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & ≈7.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the hypotenuse's length is changing at a rate of $7.1\\text{ ft/s}$ after $2$ seconds."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521462 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072e352e-d8d1-59a7-b841-bd241577a866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520774} {:answer/id #uuid "2900f8e2-5f65-5ee6-9082-48e9d9465465" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520852} {:answer/id #uuid "3a07a3c6-0949-52af-ae18-a7560b5c11e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520892} {:answer/id #uuid "94f4bb0d-5e3b-51da-bf7f-0da6c8573a76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521102} {:answer/id #uuid "bef19881-ffaa-50b7-be8d-4595b18c0067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "072e352e-d8d1-59a7-b841-bd241577a866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\text{ ft/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520774} :answer-field/id #uuid "5295ec83-ed80-5552-9033-ebac568bd564" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15178b94-38d6-597d-bc8c-39fd354b56c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-2588" :question/problem "![](images/77/7741295c73895ec39933adec100c9f3fc7e803fa8ea2a02dae543477c45cbea4.png)\n\nA boat $B$ is being pulled in toward a dock $D$ with a rope by a fisherman $F$ standing on the dock. His hands are $4\\text{ ft}$ above the ground, and he is pulling the rope at a rate of $3\\text{ ft/s}$, how fast is the distance between the dock and the boat decreasing when the boat is $3\\text{ft}$ from the dock?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/af/afbe02b63ca4d45ae4d1759d7fa452b57a8b43f073d08b1368f51472e2e4ea8b.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the dock and the boat,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground, and\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the fisherman's hands and the boat mooring.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{ft }.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & ={3}^{2}+{4}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =9+16 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{h},$\n\n- the distance between the fisherman's hands and the ground remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(0) & =2(5)(-3) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-30 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the dock and the boat is decreasing at a rate of $5\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c37f289-4fa3-5f30-a819-93edd8808892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520858} {:answer/id #uuid "3da9ef0d-595c-584c-bf88-ab4b724d3cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520899} {:answer/id #uuid "777dae30-3e3c-5423-b450-e06b7c17385d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521030} {:answer/id #uuid "af8f3c25-8d64-51cb-9a3d-fbb1ab8563c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521157} {:answer/id #uuid "d98c6aa5-05dd-5715-87aa-fa3f5ae0f286" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af8f3c25-8d64-51cb-9a3d-fbb1ab8563c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\text{ m/s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521157} :answer-field/id #uuid "447900b5-91db-51d6-8acb-33c461630cf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521358}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44d55ece-0880-515f-a521-353e6c740d6b" :question/math-academy-id "q-2942" :question/problem "The length of one leg of a right triangle increases at the rate of $3\\text{ m/s},$ while the length of the second leg is decreasing at the rate of $2\\text{ m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the hypotenuse changing when the length of the increasing leg is $12\\text{ m}$ and the length of the decreasing leg is $16\\text{ m}?$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/ee/eee3de464da57671142e2677974b44c63dc2397215ffdd2aa36e2c3d401a647d.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the length of the increasing side,\n\n- $y$ is the length of the decreasing side,\n\n- $z$ is the length of the hypotenuse.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {12}^{2}+{16}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 400 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{m}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=3\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$ when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}$  when $x=12\\,\\text{m}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(3)+2(16)(-2) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 8 & =40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{8}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the hypotenuse is changing at a rate of $\\frac{1}{5}\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319042 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3e3fbe8-493b-50a9-863c-e723e85729db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3e3fbe8-493b-50a9-863c-e723e85729db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318324} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d73eb93-a815-542a-870f-ca2e4d920490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "16cd8a89-b31e-5ada-a39c-46883ef43ef8" :question/math-academy-id "q-345644" :question/problem "Alex and Brooke agree to meet at the library. Alex arrives on time, but Brooke is late. After waiting for a while, Alex decides to leave and calls Brooke. They agree to head home instead.\n\nAlex travels north from the library at a speed of $6\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Brooke travels east away from the library. When Alex is $4\\,\\text{km}$ from the library, Brooke is $3\\,\\text{km}$ from the library. If the distance between them is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{km/h},$ what is Brooke’s speed?\n\nBrooke’s speed is \n{{field-1}}$\\text{ }\\text{km/h}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/46/46263b1e25bc520881d590ccab503ce0d6cd33e91288e81f1d678cde9a641214.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between Brooke and the library,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between Alex and the library,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between Alex and Brooke.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $x,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe know that $x=3\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=4\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {3}^{2}+{4}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 9+16 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =25 \\\\ z & =5\\,\\text{km}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=6\\,\\text{km/h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=5\\,\\text{km/h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(3)\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2(4)(6) & =2(5)(6) \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+48 & =60 \\\\ 6\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =12 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Brooke's speed is $2\\,\\text{km/h}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319055 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c9725c7b-c6e7-5a47-ac17-323beb7e67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9725c7b-c6e7-5a47-ac17-323beb7e67b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318231} :answer-field/id #uuid "144a2bd0-8d45-5119-a2f3-32a3e3b10ec9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318466}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "064c7dea-229d-541a-b55e-0089485e7b3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-346050" :question/problem "A surveyor stands $15\\text{ ft}$ away from the base of a radio tower. An elevator inside the tower moves straight up at a constant speed of $20\\text{ ft/s}.$ How fast is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator changing when the elevator is $20\\text{ ft}$ above the ground?\n\nThe distance between the surveyor and the elevator is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" :question/worked-solution "We start by drawing a diagram of the problem.\n\n![](images/7a/7a6f5b05bc0bd89d01b1b521fa35f4bc613e532a06ce04fea26323e6c5dbbafa.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the elevator and the ground,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the surveyor and the elevator.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=15\\,\\text{ft}$ and $y=20\\,\\text{ft}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {15}^{2}+{20}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ 625 & ={z}^{2} \\\\ z & =25\\,\\text{ft}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=20\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s},$\n\n- the distance between the surveyor and the base of the tower remains the same, so $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=0.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(15)(0)+2(20)(20) & =2(25)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 800 & =50\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the surveyor and the elevator is $16\\,\\text{ft}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064383 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "09a63d93-5813-5d72-8505-d2f327ab8df7" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Hypotenuse Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0517a6e8-234e-51c5-b2bd-e0d1e53c77e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326984} {:answer/id #uuid "0959f405-eb85-5237-b107-d3116cc23200" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "144\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327009} {:answer/id #uuid "4063a90a-1d64-57dc-be5e-07071a4b4ae5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "96\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327502} {:answer/id #uuid "5722aef2-837c-561c-9a7f-90a69e603283" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327691} {:answer/id #uuid "d70c2ccb-5e11-5756-952d-657013f2c226" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0517a6e8-234e-51c5-b2bd-e0d1e53c77e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\,\\text{ mph}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326984} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5087753-f0cc-5f36-bbe7-2c20e2166ba9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329501}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8393e9fb-6ea7-5c8e-84d5-5a0e41ad548b" :question/math-academy-id "q-48953" :question/problem "Two buses, $A$ and $B,$ drive toward a school. Bus $A$ travels south at a constant velocity of $30\\,\\text{ mph},$ while the bus $B$ travels west at a constant velocity of $40\\,\\text{mph}.$ If bus $A$ is $16$ miles from the school, and bus $B$ is $12$ miles from the school, how fast is the distance between the buses decreasing?" :question/worked-solution "Let's draw a diagram of the situation.\n\n![](images/07/07eb0e89a4dc85160deb45ee13e6189d32bbc905bc638cb1aee891f0504ca950.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between bus $A$ and school,\n\n- $y$ is the distance between bus $B$ and school,\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two buses.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $z,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=12\\,\\text{mi}$ and $y=16\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2} & ={x}^{2}+{y}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =(12)^{2}+(16)^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =400 \\\\ z & =20\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-40\\,\\text{mph},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-30\\,\\text{mph}.$\n\nNote that the rates of change are both negative because the distance to the school is decreasing for both buses. \n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(12)(-40)+2(16)(-30) & =2(20)\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 40\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-960-960 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{1920}{40} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =-48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the distance between the buses is decreasing at a rate of $48\\,\\text{mph}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186521459 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064382 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3497c02b-4c62-58d9-8c73-ee1a1142c429" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Leg Using the Pythagorean Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3dfb70c6-f15d-5a56-96ae-62a357522691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520902} {:answer/id #uuid "47114b29-8ab3-5f62-9aa5-c467498f9b12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "142\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520923} {:answer/id #uuid "981ee76f-3c96-5cf7-a94f-a1f50bafb998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{2}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521110} {:answer/id #uuid "bcf8f1d8-5f56-5238-805c-44a612087016" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{71}{3}\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521187} {:answer/id #uuid "e0588013-71dd-515f-af8c-61faea7d5d46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "710\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dfb70c6-f15d-5a56-96ae-62a357522691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "71\\,\\text{km/h}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b19ef90c-29be-517a-8b07-95053303a6ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186521415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0fa1eef6-69ad-599c-9e20-68839bce41b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49014" :question/problem "![](images/35/3556e6b8bda17ab8242b8cd7924e1f5ad975efcbc6c5235667d786b2f0908e1d.png)\n\nTrain $A$ travels east from station $S$ at a constant velocity of $153\\,\\text{km/h},$ and train $B$ travels south toward $S.$ The distance between the trains is increasing at a rate of $35\\text{ km/h}.$  What is the speed of train $B$ when train $A$ is $51\\,\\text{km}$ from the station and train $B$ is $68\\,\\text{km}$ from the station?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/81/81d16bb54bdecca628d769cd08d02dd327c5483851c2421fb595446c34f8a124.png)\n\nIn the diagram,\n\n- $x$ is the distance between the station and train $A,$\n\n- $y$ is the distance between the station and train $B,$\n\n- $z$ is the distance between the two trains.\n\nAs shown in the diagram, $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ form a right triangle with $z$ as the hypotenuse. We wish to find the rate of change of $y,$ which is $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ \n\nWe know that $x=51\\,\\text{km}$ and $y=68\\,\\text{km}.$ We can find $z$ using the Pythagorean Theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {51}^{2}+{68}^{2} & ={z}^{2} \\\\ {z}^{2} & =7\\,225 \\\\ z & =85\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that:\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=153\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h},$\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=35\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$\n\nFinally, we differentiate both sides of ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}={z}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ and then substitute in the known values, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({z}^{2}) \\\\ 2x\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+2y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2z\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 2(51)(153)+2(68)\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =2(85)(35) \\\\ 136\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\,950-15\\,606 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{9\\,656}{136} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-71.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of train $B$ is $71\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$"}]}
